    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $(1,b,c)$ and $(2,b,c)$ cannot be Pythagorean triples.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Neither is possible.\n\nFactoring $c^2-b^2$ gives either a zero leg or no integer value of $c$.",
          "worked": "For $(1,b,c)$,\n$$\\begin{align*}1+b^2&=c^2\\\\c^2-b^2&=1\\\\(c-b)(c+b)&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe only positive factor pair is $(1,1)$. Thus $c-b=1$ and $c+b=1$, giving $b=0$, which is not positive.\n\nFor $(2,b,c)$,\n$$\\begin{align*}4+b^2&=c^2\\\\c^2-b^2&=4\\\\(c-b)(c+b)&=4.\\end{align*}$$\nThe factor pairs are $(1,4)$ and $(2,2)$. The pair $(2,2)$ gives $b=0$. The pair $(1,4)$ gives $2c=5$, so $c$ is not an integer. Neither triple is possible.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that there is a Pythagorean triple of the form $(n,b,c)$ for all $n\\geq3$. Hint: consider the case where $n$ is even and then the case where $n$ is odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For even $n=2m$, $m\\geq2$, use $(2m,m^2-1,m^2+1)$. For odd $n=2m+1$, $m\\geq1$, use $(2m+1,2m^2+2m,2m^2+2m+1)$.\n\nChoose positive factor pairs of $n^2=(c-b)(c+b)$.",
          "worked": "We need $(c-b)(c+b)=n^2$.\n\nIf $n=2m$, where $m\\geq2$, choose $c-b=2$ and $c+b=2m^2$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}2c&=2m^2+2\\\\c&=m^2+1\\\\2b&=2m^2-2\\\\b&=m^2-1.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth are positive integers, and\n$$\\begin{align*}c^2-b^2&=(c-b)(c+b)\\\\c^2-b^2&=2(2m^2)\\\\c^2-b^2&=(2m)^2=n^2.\\end{align*}$$\nIf $n=2m+1$, where $m\\geq1$, choose $c-b=1$ and $c+b=(2m+1)^2$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}2c&=4m^2+4m+2\\\\c&=2m^2+2m+1\\\\2b&=4m^2+4m\\\\b&=2m^2+2m.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth are positive integers, and\n$$\\begin{align*}c^2-b^2&=(c-b)(c+b)\\\\c^2-b^2&=1(2m+1)^2\\\\c^2-b^2&=n^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThus every integer $n\\geq3$ occurs as a leg of a Pythagorean triple.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 164
      }
    ]
  },
